{"causes": ["The inherent instability and pervasive conflict of the Sengoku period necessitated constant strategic maneuvering for regional daimyō.", "The Tamura clan's historical control over strategically valuable lands in Mutsu Province made them a target for expansionist neighbors.", "The rise of powerful neighboring daimyō, particularly the Date clan under Date Terumune and later Date Masamune, exerted immense pressure on the Tamura domain."], "effects": ["Tamura Kiyoaki's alliance with the Date clan through his daughter's marriage significantly strengthened Date Masamune's position in northern Japan.", "The Tamura clan's territories were eventually absorbed into the larger Date domain, contributing to the formation of the powerful Sendai Domain.", "The strategic marriage between Lady Tamura and Date Masamune established a lasting dynastic connection between the two influential families."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "date-masamune", "sourceName": "Date Masamune", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "tamura-kiyoaki", "targetName": "Tamura Kiyoaki", "context": "Date Masamune's growing power significantly influenced Tamura Kiyoaki's strategic decisions, leading to their alliance in 1584."}, {"sourceSlug": "tamura-kiyoaki", "sourceName": "Tamura Kiyoaki", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "date-masamune", "targetName": "Date Masamune", "context": "Tamura Kiyoaki collaborated with Date Masamune through a strategic marital alliance, marrying his daughter to the Date clan head in 1584."}, {"sourceSlug": "tamura-kiyoaki", "sourceName": "Tamura Kiyoaki", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "mutsu-province-japan", "targetName": "Mutsu Province, Japan", "context": "Tamura Kiyoaki's primary domain and sphere of influence were located within Mutsu Province during the late 16th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "sengoku-period", "sourceName": "Sengoku Period", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "tamura-kiyoaki", "targetName": "Tamura Kiyoaki", "context": "The life and political actions of Tamura Kiyoaki are entirely contained within the historical timeframe of Japan's Sengoku Period (1467-1615)."}, {"sourceSlug": "tamura-kiyoaki", "sourceName": "Tamura Kiyoaki", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "sendai-domain", "targetName": "Sendai Domain", "context": "Tamura Kiyoaki's strategic alliance and subsequent territorial integration laid groundwork for the future establishment of the Sendai Domain under Date Masamune."}], "places": [{"name": "Miharu, Mutsu Province, Japan", "role": "Clan Base"}, {"name": "Mutsu Province, Japan", "role": "Domain"}, {"name": "Sendai, Japan", "role": "Successor Territory"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Tamura Kiyoaki", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "tamura-kiyoaki"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q11576962", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamura_Kiyoaki"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Tamura Kiyoaki's strategic leadership and his pivotal role in arranging the marriage between his daughter and Date Masamune had a significant regional impact, directly contributing to the consolidation of power in northern Japan and the rise of the Sendai Domain.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-18T08:17:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44265093408", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Tamura Kiyoaki  was a Japanese samurai and head of the Tamura clan. Tamura clan was a daimyō ruled part of Mutsu. Kiyoaki inherited the headship of the Tamura clan around 1571. His daughter Lady Tamura married Date Masamune.", "newValue": "Tamura Kiyoaki (died 1586), a prominent Japanese samurai and head of the Tamura clan, played a critical role in the tumultuous Sengoku period as a daimyō overseeing a portion of Mutsu Province. His strategic position in northern Honshu placed him at the nexus of power struggles between formidable regional lords, including the Date, Ashina, and Satake clans. Kiyoaki's significance is primarily rooted in his efforts to maintain his clan's autonomy and influence through a complex web of military maneuvers and, most notably, through a pivotal dynastic marriage that would profoundly shape the future of the region.

Kiyoaki inherited the leadership of the Tamura clan around 1571, succeeding his father, Tamura Takaaki. Facing relentless pressure from expanding neighbors, he navigated a precarious path, often shifting alliances to preserve his domain. His most consequential decision was brokering the marriage of his daughter, Lady Tamura (later known as Megohime), to Date Masamune, the ambitious and rapidly ascending young leader of the Date clan, in 1584. This alliance, forged through political necessity, aimed to secure the Tamura clan's position by linking its fate with the most powerful emerging force in northern Japan, even as it entailed a degree of subservience.

The alliance with Date Masamune, though initially a strategic safeguard, ultimately integrated the Tamura territories into the larger Date domain following Kiyoaki's death in 1586. His daughter, Megohime, became a crucial figure in Masamune's life, influencing his court and providing a direct link between the two clans. Kiyoaki's legacy is thus intrinsically tied to the consolidation of Date Masamune's power, as the Tamura territories and their strategic resources became foundational to the establishment of the Sendai Domain, one of the most powerful feudal domains of the Edo period."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-18T08:17:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44265093408", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The inherent instability and pervasive conflict of the Sengoku period necessitated constant strategic maneuvering for regional daimyō.", "The Tamura clan's historical control over strategically valuable lands in Mutsu Province made them a target for expansionist neighbors.", "The rise of powerful neighboring daimyō, particularly the Date clan under Date Terumune and later Date Masamune, exerted immense pressure on the Tamura domain."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-18T08:17:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44265093408", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1586-11-19"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1586-11-19"}], "newValue": ["Tamura Kiyoaki's alliance with the Date clan through his daughter's marriage significantly strengthened Date Masamune's position in northern Japan.", "The Tam